# Expressive Type Systems & Ergonomics: TypeScript, Kotlin, Swift
#
# Analysis of discriminated unions, control-flow narrowing, satisfies, and pragmatic type ergonomics.

#>spw_cache_lang_type_ergonomics
#:cache #!languages
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Analyze the ergonomic type system features that allow developers to model domain invariants with precision while avoiding academic rigidity."
  ~#focus: "Discriminated unions, type narrowing, the satisfies operator, extension functions, and optional chaining"
}

^["discriminated_unions_and_narrowing"]{
  ~#feature: "Discriminated Unions & Control Flow Type Narrowing"
  language: "TypeScript / Swift / Kotlin"
  syntax: "type Result = { kind: 'success', data: string } | { kind: 'error', code: number }"
  why_developers_love_it: #[
    "Natural modeling of domain states: A value is either Loaded with data, or Loading, or Errored; impossible states (e.g. data present while loading) cannot be expressed.",
    "Automatic narrowing: Checking 'if (result.kind === \"success\")' automatically narrows result to the success type within the block without type casting.",
    "Exhaustive switch checks: Assigning the default branch to 'const _exhaustive: never = result' causes the compiler to flag missed variants."
  ]
}

^["the_satisfies_operator"]{
  ~#feature: "The 'satisfies' Operator (Type Validation Without Widening)"
  language: "TypeScript 4.9+"
  syntax: "const palette = { primary: '#007ACC', secondary: '#F0F0F0' } satisfies Record<string, string>"
  why_developers_love_it: #[
    "Preserves exact literal types: Unlike 'const palette: Record<string, string>', 'satisfies' retains the exact keys 'primary' and 'secondary' for autocompletion.",
    "Catches type mismatches early: Validates that all values are valid hex strings while preventing accidental spelling mistakes in property accesses.",
    "Zero runtime artifact: Completely erased during compilation; pure static verification."
  ]
}

^["extension_functions"]{
  ~#feature: "Extension Functions & Methods"
  language: "Kotlin / Swift / C#"
  syntax: "fun String.toSnakeCase(): String = ..."
  why_developers_love_it: #[
    "Fluent API design: Enables dot-chaining 'user.name.toSnakeCase()' without forcing the method into the original class definition.",
    "Zero prototype pollution: Resolved statically at compile time; does not mutate global prototypes or affect external libraries.",
    "Cleaner than static utility classes: Banishes ugly utility wrappers like 'StringUtils.toSnakeCase(user.name)'."
  ]
}

^["optional_chaining_and_null_coalescing"]{
  ~#feature: "Optional Chaining (?.) and Null Coalescing (??)"
  language: "TypeScript / JavaScript / Swift / C#"
  syntax: "const avatar = user?.profile?.settings?.avatar ?? defaultAvatar"
  why_developers_love_it: #[
    "Extinguishes null-pointer exceptions: Safely navigates deeply nested, optional object graphs without defensive 'if (user && user.profile)' ladders.",
    "Short-circuiting semantics: Halts navigation immediately at the first null or undefined link.",
    "Expressive defaults: Provides clean fallback values when an optional property is absent."
  ]
}

^["taste"]{
  ~#taste: "Types should serve as guardrails for creativity, not handcuffs that force ceremonial paperwork."
  ~#wonder: "Why did TypeScript become the most popular typing system in the world despite being deliberately unsound?"

  principles: #[
    "Pragmatism over purity: Modeling real-world JavaScript idioms was more valuable than academic completeness.",
    "Types as documentation: Reading a well-crafted type signature explains the entire contract of a function without looking at its body.",
    "Zero runtime penalty: Types exist to protect the developer during authoring, then completely vanish at runtime."
  ]
}

^["invariants"]{
  "Discriminated union narrowing preserves type narrowing across nested condition scopes.",
  "The satisfies operator never widens property keys or value literal types.",
  "Extension functions compile to static method dispatches without runtime lookup overhead.",
}

#>wonder_lang_type_ergonomics_1
?["How does 'making impossible states untypeable' change an engineering team's bug triage burden?"]{
  #:depth #!philosophical ~#lens(defensive_elimination)
  ~#hypothesis: "When domain models use discriminated unions rather than multiple optional booleans, entire categories of edge-case bugs vanish before code review."
  !probe{ "Audit 50 production frontend bugs; count how many were caused by invalid state combinations (e.g., isLoading=true but error=true) that a union would have prevented." }
  $%[bugs.impossible_state_ratio, developer.debugging_time_reduction]
}
